  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAnother busy night across much of the area. A wound-up closed \nupper low over Arizona is slowly lifting to the northeast, with a\nnoticeable dry slot keeping southwestern New Mexico quiet tonight.\nAn 80+ knot subtropical jet stream nosing into northern Sonora up\ntowards southern New Mexico, with strong upper level divergence\nacross our area. This, combined with weak upper level impulses \nembedded in southwest flow aloft, has led to several organized\nrounds of thunderstorms with torrential rain, all arcing into\nindividual MCSs. The first two managed to more or less split\naround El Paso City, but are more or less merged together again,\narcing from southern Sierra County, down the Tularosa Basin, and \njust finally shifting east of the El Paso-Hudspeth County line.\nExpect storms over Sierra County to weaken, with upscale growth\nacross Otero and Hudspeth County. Another significant cluster of\nstorms near Ahumada in Chihuahua will lift into south-central \nHudspeth County after midnight. There's still an outside chance of\na few storms popping up over El Paso in the next couple hours, as\nweak outflow boundaries from the earlier pre-merged complexes \nmeet, but with the best upper level support shifting east, it's a \nfairly low chance.\n\nDrier air aloft will result in lower thunderstorm coverage on\nFriday, especially in the central part of the CWA. The upper low\nin Arizona will graze northern New Mexico as a weakened trough\nduring the afternoon, and a trailing vort lobe will work into\nOtero and Hudspeth Counties in the afternoon. Low level moisture\nwill still be fairly deep near and east of the Rio Grande,\ncontributing to moderate instability (SBCAPE 1000-1500 J/Kg).\nExpect we will still see isolated to widely scattered \nthunderstorms in the afternoon, some of them approaching severe \nlimits with 25-30 knots of deep layer shear lingering. Sierra, \nOtero, and Hudspeth counties will be slightly favored.\n\nMuch quieter weather is on tap for the weekend.\n\nDeep subtropical moisture will get pulled back into the area on\nMonday, running out well ahead of 'Polo', while an upper low drops\ninto southern California again. Polo looks to track across Baja\nCalifornia and across the Sierra Madres towards us or the Big Bend\narea. The dynamics aloft associated with Polo are the bigger\nplayer than the moisture, which mostly gets washed out over Baja\nand the Sierra Madres. The GFS shows a modest jump in PWAT around\nEl Paso Tuesday afternoon, from about 1.45 to 1.60 inches. The\nlatter is a high-end value for us, but Polo or not, there will be\nplenty of moisture around for additional heavy rainfall. The\ndynamics aloft, as Polo weakens into an upper trough and strong\nvort max, are perhaps more impressive than its moisture potential.\n500mb flow ahead of its remnant trough increases to 50-65 knots,\nso the severe potential shouldn't be downplayed. Right now, that\narea looks to mostly stay east and southeast of El Paso, but will\nneed to be carefully watched. Otherwise, the return of\nthunderstorms with torrential rainfall will be the main concern\nMonday-Tuesday Night. Dry slotting on the south side of the\n\"kicker\" low will clear out the moisture for Wednesday. The 00Z\nGFS even looks a bit dry and breezy as lee troughing merges with\nwhatever is left of Polo's low level reflection.",
